activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T16:53:24.8006243Z 
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T16:53:24.8006458Z To create a pull request:
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T16:53:24.8007029Z 1. Make any file changes directly in the working directory.
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T16:53:24.8008124Z 2. If you haven't done so already, create a new local branch using `git checkout -b <branch-name>` with an appropriate unique name.
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T16:53:24.8009994Z 3. Add and commit your changes to the branch. Be careful to add exactly the files you intend, and check there are no extra files left un-added. Verify you haven't deleted or changed any files you didn't intend to.
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T16:53:24.8011488Z 4. Do not push your changes. That will be done by the tool.
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T16:53:24.8012643Z 5. Create the pull request with the create_pull_request tool from safeoutputs exactly once, only when the final title/body/branch are ready.
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T16:53:24.8014805Z 6. After calling `create_pull_request`, STOP. Do not probe with another PR call, do not test auth with manual `git push`, and do not rewrite git remotes.
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T16:53:24.8015738Z 
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T16:53:24.8018402Z **Important**: The `branch` parameter in the create_pull_request tool **must exactly match the name of your current local git branch** — the branch you just committed to. You can verify this with `git branch --show-current`. Never invent or guess a branch name; always use the actual branch name from `git branch --show-current`. If you are on an existing branch (e.g. you checked out a PR branch), use that branch name.
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T16:53:24.8020719Z 
activation	Print prompt	2026-08-24T16:53:24.8022309Z **Important**: `create_pull_request` is not a sandbox. Every successful call records a real PR intent that can become an externally visible pull request. Never use placeholder payloads like `"test"` / `"test"` to see what happens. If you are not ready to open the real PR, use `noop` or `report_incomplete` instead.
